{"authored" [{"question" "q-326251" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curve with parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{\\,t+4\\,},\\,y=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}-t,\\,-3\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | {{field-1}} | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $2$ | $\\sqrt{5}$ | $\\sqrt{6}$ |\n| $y$ | $\\frac{33}{2}$ | $8$ | {{field-2}} | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | {{field-3}} |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038739/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec9150058e1d0d66afb3809401644156d5239c7298e4d287d0a47f4f3ddf4705" "basis" 1343 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038739/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-326251" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "- To find the value of $x(-3),$ we substitute $t=-3$ into $x=\\sqrt{\\,t+4\\,}.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(-3)=\\sqrt{-3+4}=\\sqrt{1}=1.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $y(-1),$ we substitute $t=-1$ into $y=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}-t.$ This gives\n\n$$\ny(-1)=\\frac{3}{2}(-1)^{2}-(-1)=\\frac{3}{2}+1=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{2}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $y(2),$ we substitute $t=2$ into $y=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}-t.$ This gives\n\n$$\ny(2)=\\frac{3}{2}(2)^{2}-(2)=6-2=4.\n$$\n\nThen the table is \n\n \n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $2$ | $\\sqrt{5}$ | $\\sqrt{6}$ |\n| $y$ | $\\frac{33}{2}$ | $8$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $4$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038739/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec9150058e1d0d66afb3809401644156d5239c7298e4d287d0a47f4f3ddf4705" "basis" 1343 "ve