8874890bfbd8e1f55c5ec6d04df123712c"} {"question" "q-73626" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073429/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a8bf6720b70a127fff563e34563a58874890bfbd8e1f55c5ec6d04df123712c"} {"question" "q-73626" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ 2 & \\text{ }\\ge -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\text{ }\\ge -2 \\\\ -2 & \\le -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 2 \\\\ 1-2 & \\le 1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1+2 \\\\ -1 & \\le x\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-1\\le x\\le 3.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -2 & \\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 2 \\\\ -2-3 & \\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3\\le 2-3 \\\\ -5 & \\le y\\le -1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-5\\le y\\le -1.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $-1\\le x\\le 3$ and $-5\\le y\\le -1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073429/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a8bf6720b70a127fff563e34563a58874890bfbd8e1f55c5ec6d04df123712c"} {"question" "q-73626" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/que