e x\\le 6$ and $-13\\le y\\le -1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073429/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a8bf6720b70a127fff563e34563a58874890bfbd8e1f55c5ec6d04df123712c"} {"question" "q-70523" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073429/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c59abd7d6f673481b73098c3b0b57ad19b4515f7214a4c3759965303bb4b5ee6"} {"question" "q-70523" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073429/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a8bf6720b70a127fff563e34563a58874890bfbd8e1f55c5ec6d04df123712c"} {"question" "q-70523" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2\\le x\\le 13,\\,3\\le y\\le 7"] ["math" "-6\\le x\\le 0,\\,1\\le y\\le 7"] ["math" "0\\le x\\le 5,\\,-7\\le y\\le 0"] ["math" "1\\le x\\le 5,\\,-9\\le y\\le -2"] ["math" "4\\le x\\le 6,\\,-13\\le y\\le -1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073429/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a8bf6720b70a127fff563e34563a58874890bfbd8e1f55c5ec6d04df123712c"} {"question" "q-70523" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4\\le x\\le 6,\\,-13\\le y\\le -1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073429/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a8bf6720b70a127fff563e34563a58874890bfbd8e1f55c5ec6d04df123712c"} {"question" "e-5075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=3t, \\,\\,\\, y=t^3, \\quad -3\\leq t \\leq 3,\n$$\n\nfill in the following table of values.\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073429/example-5075.json" "file_sha256" "03768d879659e8a9df614cacdb82366b9c9edc06a1cdfd03f40dbf65094d70f9"} {"question" "e-5075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=3t.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-9$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we work out the $y$-values using $y=t^3.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-9$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| $y$ | $-27$ | $-8$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $8$ | $27$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073429/example-5075.json" "file_sha256" "03768d879659e8a9df614cacdb82366b9c9edc06a1cdfd03f40dbf65094d70f9"} {"question" "e-5098" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Plot the graph of the parametrically defined curve\n\n$$\nx=t(t-6), \\,\\,\\, y=t^3, \\quad -2\\leq t \\leq 2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073429/example-5098.json" "file_sha256" "3a5519f4878a06bb2070df20420f62dfe82dbec51f01dcb5a7b10c2b0949727d"} {"question" "e-5098" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=t(t-6).$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $16$ | $7$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-8$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=t^3.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $16$ | $7$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-8$ |\n| $y$ | $-8$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $8$ |\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073429/assets/f15f25af0c4804dc811a0ab231ba2ef3ecce2ecd12501a1f2d8b9e34b71a505b.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073429/example-5098.json" "file_sha256" "3a5519f4878a06bb2070df20420f62dfe82dbec51f01dcb5a7b10c2b0949727d"} {"question" "e-5099" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t^2-6t, \\,\\,\\, y=t^4+4, \\quad t \\in (-\\infty , \\infty),\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073429/example-5099.json" "file_sha256" "c80c4c7750eedb8e264e8471f8f91fb953195b71985270eb7c15c7f5f915a92e"} {"question" "e-5099" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ we can start by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={t}^{2}-6t \\\\  & ={(t-3)}^{2}-(3)^{2} \\\\  & ={(t-3)}^{2}-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\left( t-3\\right)^2 \\geq 0,$ we can conclude that $x \\geq -9.$\n\n- To find the range of $y=t^4+4,$ notice that $t^4 \\geq 0,$ and consequently $y \\geq 4.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $x \\geq -9$ and $y \\geq 4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073429/example-5099.json" "file_sha256" "c80c4c7750eedb8e264e8471f8f91fb953195b71985270eb7c15c7f5f915a92e"} {"question" "e-5292" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx = 4\\cos\\theta, \\,\\,\\, y = 2\\sin\\theta, \\quad 0\\leq \\theta <     2\\pi,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073429/example-5292.json" "file_sha256" "2469c5b7dd232838ea64132a7c59359a235481b5fd0446f270cf2ee172bc04c6"} {"question" "e-5292" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\leq\\theta <  2\\pi,$ we have $-1 \\leq \\cos{\\theta} \\leq 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -4 & \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 4 \\\\ -4 & \\le x\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-4 \\leq x \\leq 4.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\leq\\theta <  2\\pi,$ we have $-1 \\leq \\sin{\\theta} \\leq 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -2 & \\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 2 \\\\ -2 & \\le y\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-2 \\leq y \\leq 2.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $-4 \\leq x \\leq 4$ and $-2 \\leq y \\leq 2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073429/example-5292.json" "file_sha256" "2469c5b7dd232838ea64132a7c59359a235481b5fd0446f270cf2ee172bc04c6"}] "usage" {"q-16190" [] "q-70523" [] "e-5098" [] "e-5292" [] "e-5099" [] "q-326251" [] "e-5075" [] "q-16196" []} "basis" 2744 "retractions" [[17592186301639 :question/worked-solution "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$ This gives\n\n| **$\\theta $** | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ | $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ | $\\pi $ | $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ | $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $2$ | $\\s